/// What the transport says the world should be doing.
/// 
/// `state` is the `PlayState` discriminant rather than the enum, so this
/// protocol does not force a serde derive onto a type whose whole point is
/// to be two atomics read from a render loop.
/// 
/// `debug` rides with it because it is part of the same press. The play
/// bar has two boot buttons and the only difference between them is that
/// flag, which the editor used to set in a static the linked game read
/// directly. Out of process that static is in the wrong address space, so
/// Debug booted the ordinary configuration and looked identical to Play.
Transport { state: u8, debug: bool },
/// Run exactly one pass while paused.
Step,
/// The Game panel is this many physical pixels now.
/// 
/// The game answers by allocating a surface and sending `Surface` back. It
/// is not required to answer immediately, and the editor keeps drawing the
/// old surface until it does — a resize that blanked the panel for a frame
/// would flicker on every drag of a dock splitter.
Resize { width: u32, height: u32 },
/// What a person did, in the picture's own coordinates.
/// 
/// A batch and not one message per event: a pointer moving across the
/// panel produces one of these per frame, and a JSON line each would be
/// the per-frame traffic this protocol is built to avoid.
Input { events: Vec<input::InputEvent> },
/// Whether keys are aimed at the Game panel.
/// 
/// The dock already knows this and the game cannot: the game's window is
/// not the window the person is typing into.
Focus { has: bool },
/// Whether the Game panel is on screen at all.
/// 
/// THE ONE FACT THE GAME CANNOT WORK OUT AND PAYS THE MOST FOR
/// ----------------------------------------------------------
/// A hosted game renders into a surface, so nothing on its side ever goes
/// dark. Its window is hidden by construction, the surface it draws into
/// is a page of memory, and both stay exactly as valid when the panel is
/// behind a sibling tab as when it is in front. So the game rendered at
/// sixty hertz — the scene pass, the post chain, the CRT filter and the
/// publish blit — into an image the editor was not sampling, for as long
/// as a person left the Tickets board raised.
/// 
/// The editor has the fact and had nowhere to put it. `game_view_rect` is
/// published as a side effect of the panel drawing and expires when it
/// stops, which is already the exact question; `pump_hostlink` read it,
/// printed one line about it and sent nothing.
/// 
/// A want, on the change, like `Console` and `Profile`. It differs from
/// those three in which way it fails: a want that never arrives leaves
/// those panels empty, and a want that never arrives here leaves the game
/// RENDERING. That is deliberate — the game defaults to visible, so an
/// older editor, a dropped line and a detach all fall back to the
/// behaviour that existed before this message.
Visible { want: bool },
/// How many physical pixels the panel puts in one layout unit.
/// 
/// `Resize` carries PHYSICAL PIXELS and always has. A renderer that lays a
/// document out cannot use that number alone: it has to know how much of
/// it is display density and how much is room. Given 1600x1000 on a retina
/// display and no scale, a page reflows as though it had 1600 CSS pixels
/// of width and every 16px font on it lands at 8 points — half the size of
/// the label beside it in the editor's own chrome.
/// 
/// This is the same arithmetic the native views do with `zoom`, sent
/// rather than computed, because the browser child is the one process that
/// cannot see the window.
/// 
/// A GAME IGNORES IT, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. A game renders a
/// scene, not a document: it wants every pixel it was given and has no
/// layout to reflow. The guest reads this arm and does nothing, so the
/// meaning stays "for a renderer that lays out text".
/// 
/// On the change, like `Resize` and `Visible`. It moves when a window is
/// dragged to a display of a different density, which is rarely.
Scale { factor: f32 },
/// Go to this address.
/// 
/// A GAME IGNORES IT, for the reason it ignores `Scale`: a game has no
/// address. The arm is read and dropped on that side.
/// 
/// Without this the browser child could only ever show the page it was
/// built with, which made every panel above it a browser that cannot
/// browse. It is a message and not an argument to the launch because a
/// panel navigates over and over and starts a process once.
Navigate { url: String },
/// Move in the history the child is keeping, or load the page again.
/// 
/// Separate from `Navigate` because none of these three has an address.
/// The history belongs to the browser and this side does not have it — the
/// native route infers Back and Forward from a `TabSet` it keeps beside
/// the view, and a composited child has the real thing.
/// 
/// A GAME IGNORES IT, as it ignores `Scale` and `Navigate`.
Browse { action: Browse },
/// Whether a profiler is open on the editor's side.
/// 
/// It carries a want and not a "start"/"stop" pair, because the state that
/// matters is whether anybody is LOOKING, and an editor that crashed with
/// a profiler open would never send the stop. The game re-arms a clock
/// from each of these and the instrumentation lapses on its own when they
/// stop arriving — the census's brake, over a socket.
Profile { want: bool },
/// Whether a Performance panel is open on the editor's side, showing the
/// GAME rather than the editor.
/// 
/// The panel this arms had the same failure as the census and the console,
/// and hid it better than either. Its four figures come from statics the
/// render loop fills — `RENDER_DRAW_US` and the two beside it, plus the
/// scene pass's instance counter — and out of process every one of them is
/// filled by the EDITOR'S render loop. So the panel did not go blank. It
/// drew the editor's own chrome, at a steady sixty, under a status line
/// reading "game running", and the only visible symptom was a population
/// of zero on a world with sixty thousand instances in it.
/// 
/// A want and not a start/stop, re-armed per frame on the game's side,
/// exactly as `Profile` is and for the same reason: an editor that died
/// with the panel open would never send the stop.
Perf { want: bool },
/// How the game image should look: the HDR chain, the CRT filter, the
/// anti-aliasing, the two depth-of-field passes and the lighting model.
/// 
/// The Post-processing section edits one global, and it used to be enough
/// that it did. Every viewport in the process read it and the picture
/// changed on the next frame. Out of process the picture is composed by
/// the CHILD, off the child's own copy of that global — which starts at
/// `DisplaySettings::DEFAULT` and had no writer. So a person turning
/// bloom down watched the editor's own idea of bloom change and the game
/// keep exactly the look it booted with.
/// 
/// THE SERDE DERIVE THAT `Transport` REFUSED
/// -----------------------------------------
/// `Transport` sends the `PlayState` discriminant rather than the enum,
/// on the grounds that this protocol should not force serde onto an
/// engine type. That reasoning does not reach here and the difference is
/// the type, not the rule: `PlayState` is two atomics read from a render
/// loop, and `DisplaySettings` is a POD of `bool` and `f32` that is
/// already copied out from under a mutex every time anything reads it.
/// Restating forty fields in a wire struct would buy nothing but a second
/// place for a new field to be forgotten.
/// 
/// Sent on the CHANGE, like `Resize` and unlike a frame. A drag on a
/// slider therefore costs one line per frame while the pointer is down
/// and nothing at all afterwards, which is the same traffic `Input`
/// already makes and for the same reason.
Display { settings: crate::types::DisplaySettings },
/// What the Hierarchy is asking the world for.
/// 
/// The census channel is a set of process globals, and the Hierarchy's
/// half of it wrote every one of them in the wrong process. `want_census`
/// is the load-bearing case: the publisher's first line is a check on it,
/// so a game whose copy nobody had ever set returned before building a
/// single row. The panel drew "the world publishes no census" over a world
/// with forty thousand things in it.
/// 
/// All three fields are the panel's ASK and not its state, which is why
/// they travel together and are applied together. `want` re-arms the
/// publisher's brake the way `Profile` does. `expanded` is what stops a
/// census being forty thousand rows — the publisher sends full rows for
/// open nodes and a count for the rest, and a publisher told nothing sends
/// everything. `inspect` is the subject, carried because the Inspector's
/// row is open whatever the tree says and a publisher that did not know
/// which row that was would leave that panel permanently blank.
/// 
/// Sent on the CHANGE, like `Display`. Opening a node is a click and a
/// hover is a frame, so the traffic is a person's hand and not the clock.
Census {
    want: bool,
    expanded: Vec<crate::panels::census::EntityId>,
    inspect: crate::panels::census::EntityId,
},
/// What a person did to a row: clicks, edited values, the hover.
/// 
/// SEPARATE FROM `Census` BECAUSE ONE IS A LEVEL AND ONE IS AN EVENT
/// ----------------------------------------------------------------
/// The ask above is idempotent — resending it changes nothing, and a
/// dropped one is corrected by the next. These are not. A click delivered
/// twice selects twice, and a click delivered never does nothing at all,
/// so this message is sent when there is something in it and the queues it
/// drains are emptied by the sending. Folding them into one message would
/// force the ask to be re-sent on every frame that had a click in it, or
/// the clicks to be held until the ask changed.
/// 
/// `highlight` rides here rather than above because it is the same
/// pointer's doing, and 0 means "over nothing" exactly as it does in the
/// channel it is going into.
CensusInput {
    requests: Vec<crate::panels::census::Request>,
    edits: Vec<crate::panels::census::Edit>,
    highlight: crate::panels::census::EntityId,
},
/// Whether a Debug Console panel is open in the editor.
/// 
/// The console channel is process globals, like the census one next door,
/// and it failed in the same place first: the game's publisher opens with
/// `if !console_wanted() { return }`, and nothing in the game's process
/// had ever armed that. So the game did not merely fail to send its
/// scrollback — it never built one, and the panel drew "0 lines" against a
/// console with a screenful of output in it.
/// 
/// A want and not a start/stop, re-armed on the game's side every frame,
/// exactly as `Profile` is. Sent on the CHANGE: a panel is opened and
/// closed by a person, so this is two lines a session and not sixty a
/// second.
Console { want: bool },
/// Commands typed into that panel, oldest first.
/// 
/// SEPARATE FROM `Console` FOR THE REASON `CensusInput` IS SEPARATE
/// ---------------------------------------------------------------
/// The want above is a level and resending it changes nothing. A command
/// is an event: delivered twice it runs twice, and `cd learn` before `ls`
/// is not the same session as after it. So this carries the drained queue
/// in order, and is sent only on the frames a person pressed Return.
/// 
/// The engine still never learns what a console command is. This is a
/// String on a socket, handed to `devconsole::submit` at the far end and
/// run by whatever the game runs commands with.
Command { lines: Vec<String> },
/// Rebinds made on the Control Map, for the game to perform.
/// 
/// THE GAME OWNS THE TABLE, AND IT HAS TO
/// -------------------------------------
/// This is the one panel whose edits cannot be applied where they are
/// made. The binding table is what the game's INPUT PASS reads — `held`
/// and `pressed` are called from the game's own frame — so a rebind that
/// changed only the editor's copy would repaint the board correctly and
/// leave the key doing what it did before. Every other panel here carries
/// a want or a level; this carries an instruction.
/// 
/// It is also why the store stays on the game's side. Both processes read
/// the same file from the same config directory, and only the game has the
/// catalog that says which lines in it are live.
/// 
/// A queue and not one edit, for the reason `Command` carries one: a
/// rebind is an EVENT. Two clicks are two rebinds, and Reset All followed
/// by a single rebind is not the same table as the other order.
Rebind { edits: Vec<crate::panels::bindings::Edit> },
/// The machine is low on memory, and this is the editor asking for some of
/// it back before anyone is stopped for it.
/// 
/// NOT A CAP, AND THE GUARANTEE DOES NOT REST ON THE GAME OBEYING
/// -------------------------------------------------------------
/// The editor watches how much memory the MACHINE has left, not how much
/// this game holds, and sends this only when the reserve it keeps for
/// itself and the OS is at risk. `Warn` asks the game to shed what it can;
/// the game stays up. `Critical` is the last word before `Shutdown` and a
/// kill, sent so a game with unsaved state gets the chance the X button
/// gives it. A game that ignores this is doing nothing wrong — shedding
/// only moves the stop further away, and the editor can always stop it.
MemoryPressure { level: MemoryPressureLevel },
/// Save and exit. Sent before the editor resorts to killing the process,
/// so a game with unsaved state gets the chance the X button would give it.
Shutdown,

/// Attached, and here is who. The pid is for the editor's own diagnostics:
/// a game that hangs is killed by pid, and the editor should not have to
/// ask the OS what it just spawned.
Ready { pid: u32 },
/// The look this game booted with.
/// 
/// Both processes start their own copy of `DisplaySettings` at
/// `DisplaySettings::DEFAULT`, which is every effect off, and only the game
/// knows what its picture is supposed to be. The editor answers `Ready`
/// with a `ToGame::Display`, so without this the editor's untouched panel
/// told a game with an HDR chain and a CRT filter to turn both off, on the
/// frame it attached, every session — and Stella Nova came up flat under
/// the editor and graded from a shell.
/// 
/// Sent BEFORE `Ready`, and the order is the whole contract: the editor
/// decides what to send while it handles `Ready`, so it has to be holding
/// this already. One socket carries the two in order.
/// 
/// A first look and not a running one. The editor owns the settings once a
/// game is up, exactly as the Post-processing section says it does, and the
/// game never sends this again.
Display { settings: crate::types::DisplaySettings },
/// A surface exists and is published under this name.
/// 
/// Sent on first frame and after every resize. The dimensions ride along
/// because the host needs them to build a texture and reading them back
/// off the surface would be a second source of truth for one fact.
Surface {
    name: String,
    width: u32,
    height: u32,
},
/// The game could not make a surface. The editor keeps showing the boot
/// terminal, which is where a reason belongs.
NoSurface { why: String },
/// The scope rings exist and are published under this name.
/// 
/// Sent once, at attach, and never again — the rings are a fixed-size
/// block that no resize or device change reallocates, so unlike `Surface`
/// there is nothing later to correct.
/// 
/// THE SAMPLES ARE NOT IN THIS MESSAGE, AND CANNOT BE
/// --------------------------------------------------
/// This carries a name for the same reason `Surface` does: what is being
/// shared is too big and too frequent for the channel. A frame does not
/// travel here and neither does a waveform — two lanes at 44.1 kHz is a
/// third of a megabyte a second before JSON turns each float into decimal
/// text. The editor maps the page instead and reads the rings the audio
/// callback is already filling.
Audio { name: String },
/// A quarter second of the game's frames, named span by span.
/// 
/// THIS IS NOT THE PER-FRAME MESSAGE THIS FILE REFUSES
/// ---------------------------------------------------
/// The header above says a frame does not travel on this channel and that
/// the channel carries only what changes state. A span tree at sixty hertz
/// would be exactly the traffic it rules out, so this is not one: the game
/// reduces a WINDOW of frames to a single Report and sends four a second.
/// 
/// What that buys is the reading the Performance panel cannot give. Its
/// timing atomics are filled by whichever process fills them, so in the
/// editor they are the editor's own chrome — the game's frame is in
/// another address space and no clock here starts when its work does. A
/// Report is the game's own instrumentation, measured where the work is
/// and named where the names are.
/// 
/// Sent only while the editor has asked for it. See `ToGame::Profile`.
Profile { report: crate::panels::profile::Report },
/// The game's own frames, phase by phase, in a batch.
/// 
/// WHY A BATCH AND NOT A FRAME, AND WHY IT IS NOT THE PROFILER'S JOB
/// -----------------------------------------------------------------
/// The Performance panel's unit is one column per frame — that is what
/// makes a spike one column wide and findable — so unlike a Report this
/// cannot be reduced at the source without becoming a different panel. It
/// is still not a per-frame message: the game accumulates its columns and
/// sends a quarter second of them at once, on `Profile`'s cadence, which
/// is four lines a second carrying fifteen samples of four floats each.
/// 
/// A `Sample` is sixteen bytes of numbers and the panel's own type. It
/// crosses as itself for the reason a `Report` does — one type means the
/// panel has one renderer rather than two that drift, and the local path
/// exercises the wire format every session.
/// 
/// The counts ride along rather than travelling on their own, because a
/// population and the frames it was drawn in are one reading. They are the
/// LAST scene pass's and not one per sample: the counter is a global the
/// pass overwrites, so there is no per-frame history of it to send, and
/// claiming one by pairing the current counts with each sample would
/// fabricate fifteen readings from one.
/// 
/// Sent only while the editor has asked for it. See `ToGame::Perf`.
Perf {
    samples: Vec<crate::panels::perf::Sample>,
    counts: Option<crate::render::InstanceCounts>,
},
/// What the world says exists, as the publisher built it.
/// 
/// THIS ONE IS BIG, AND THE BRAKE IS ALREADY IN THE CHANNEL
/// -------------------------------------------------------
/// A census is rows of Strings, and Stella Nova's selectables cache runs
/// to about forty-two thousand of them. That is not something to put on a
/// socket, and it is not what is on it: the channel was built inverted for
/// this exact cost, and a publisher sends full rows for the nodes the
/// panel has OPEN and a bare COUNT for everything else. A live census is
/// tens of rows, at the publisher's own rate of ten a second — the same
/// order as `Profile`, and for the same reason it was allowed.
/// 
/// The generation inside it is the PUBLISHER'S and is installed verbatim.
/// The editor's own counter would be a number the game has never issued,
/// and every click drawn from this snapshot would name it and be refused
/// as aged out — a hierarchy that lists the world correctly and does
/// nothing when a person clicks it.
/// 
/// The other two fields are the publisher's half of a two-way channel and
/// have to arrive with the rows they name. `inspect` is a CORRECTION: ids
/// are indices into a table the publisher rebuilds, so the row the
/// Inspector is holding is renumbered under it on every publish, and the
/// publisher owes it the id that same object has now. `reveal` is a
/// one-shot — a selection made in the game window, which the panel answers
/// by scrolling to it once.
Census {
    census: crate::panels::census::Census,
    inspect: crate::panels::census::EntityId,
    reveal: crate::panels::census::EntityId,
},
/// The game's console scrollback, whole.
/// 
/// WHY THE WHOLE BUFFER AND NOT THE NEW LINES
/// ------------------------------------------
/// An append-only stream is the build that looks right until somebody
/// types `cls`. A scrollback is also CLEARED and also DROPS ITS OLDEST
/// LINES at its cap, and neither is an append — so a panel fed appends
/// keeps showing lines the game no longer has, permanently and with
/// nothing on screen to say so. The channel's unit is a snapshot for that
/// reason, and putting a diff on the wire would reintroduce here exactly
/// what the channel refused.
/// 
/// It is affordable because it is rare: the game only builds a snapshot
/// when its revision moved, which is when a command ran or something
/// printed. An idle game sends nothing at all.
/// 
/// `revision` is the GAME'S counter and is installed verbatim, like the
/// census generation. It is compared for equality and never interpreted,
/// so a game that restarts and counts from zero again is not a problem —
/// the editor's copy of it belongs to whichever game is attached.
Console {
    revision: u64,
    lines: Vec<crate::devconsole::Line>,
},
/// Every word Tab can complete to, from the game's command table.
/// 
/// Sent once per game, because a command table is fixed at build time. It
/// is a separate message rather than a field on `Console` so that the
/// snapshot — the frequent one — does not carry a few hundred words that
/// never change on every print.
ConsoleWords { words: Vec<String> },
/// The game's binding table, whole: every action it declared and every
/// override the person has on it.
/// 
/// WHAT THIS FIXES IS THE PANEL'S EMPTY STATE BEING RIGHT
/// -----------------------------------------------------
/// `bindings::register` fills a process static, and the only caller is the
/// application that owns the controls. Out of process that call happens in
/// the game, and the Control Map reads the editor's static — so the panel
/// drew "no controls declared" over a game with a full scheme in it. It
/// was not wrong; there genuinely were no controls declared in the process
/// doing the drawing. This is the pattern the header names, one more time.
/// 
/// Sent at attach and then on every change of the game's own revision,
/// which is a rebind — one line per click of a key chip, and nothing at
/// all while a game is merely running. A catalog is a hundred actions of
/// short strings, so the snapshot is sent whole for the reason the console
/// scrollback is: a diff would have to describe a reset, and a reset is a
/// removal.
/// 
/// It carries the OVERRIDES as well as the catalog, and must. The editor
/// could load the same file itself — it is one config directory — but it
/// cannot tell which of those lines belong to actions the game declares
/// without the catalog it is being sent anyway.
Controls { snapshot: crate::panels::bindings::Snapshot },
/// Which screen the game is on, in the game's own words.
/// 
/// THIS IS THE JOIN THE STORYBOARD WAS MISSING
/// -------------------------------------------
/// A storyboard names its screens twice: a title for people, and a
/// `state_id` for "what the code calls it". The second was documentation
/// with no reader — nothing could check it, and nothing could use it. This
/// message is what it is for. The game says `title_screen`, the editor
/// photographs the frame, and the card whose `state_id` is `title_screen`
/// shows what the screen actually looks like.
/// 
/// It is a message and not a global for the reason this whole file
/// exists: the game's phase is a value in the game's address space, and
/// the panel that wants it draws in the editor's.
/// 
/// SENT ON CHANGE, WHICH IS THE ONLY TIME IT SAYS ANYTHING. A game sits on
/// one screen for seconds at a time, so this is a handful of lines per
/// session — not the per-frame traffic the header refuses.
Screen { id: String },
/// The game is about to die, and here is why, in the game's own words.
/// 
/// WHY, WHEN THE EXIT STATUS ALREADY SAYS THE GAME DIED
/// ---------------------------------------------------
/// The editor already learns a game left, from `try_wait`, and already
/// prints the status. "exited 101" or "killed by signal 11" tells a person
/// that something broke and nothing about what. This carries the panic
/// message, the line it fired on and a backtrace, so the crash reaches the
/// person who must fix it instead of a log they must go and find.
/// 
/// Sent by the game's panic hook, which runs where it can still allocate and
/// still reach the socket. A fatal SIGNAL cannot send this — a signal
/// handler may not allocate — so a segfault leaves a marker in a file the
/// editor reads instead. One `CrashReport` carries both, and the editor
/// builds a third kind itself when it is the one doing the killing.
Crash { report: CrashReport },
/// The game's live memory footprint, as the game's own allocator counts it.
/// 
/// The editor watches the machine's free memory to decide WHEN to act; that
/// number cannot say how much of the pressure is this game. This can — the
/// bytes the game's global allocator holds — sent on a coarse cadence so a
/// meter can show it and a warning can name the game rather than guess.
/// 
/// A LEVEL: a later reading replaces a waiting one rather than piling up.
/// See `supersedes`.
Mem { bytes: u64, peak: u64 },

/// Pointer position in GAME IMAGE pixels. See the header.
PointerMoved { x: f32, y: f32 },
/// The pointer left the picture. Not the window: leaving the panel is what
/// the game must hear, and it happens far more often.
PointerGone,
Button { button: MouseButton, state: ElementState },
/// Lines, not pixels. A trackpad's pixel delta is converted at the tap, so
/// the child replays one kind and the game reads one kind.
Scroll { dx: f32, dy: f32 },
Key {
    code: Option<VirtualKeyCode>,
    scancode: u32,
    state: ElementState,
},
/// A typed character. Separate from `Key` because winit reports it
/// separately and egui needs it: a layout that produces "@" from two keys
/// cannot be reconstructed from the key events.
Text { ch: char },
Modifiers { mods: ModifiersState },

/// Engine-side name of the atlas. It is the `name` argument of
/// `MegaAtlas::load_multi`, which makes it the base name of the `.pak`
/// file the native path looks for, and the `PAK_STORE` key the browser
/// path looks up.
pub id: String,
/// Slot number. Registration sorts by it, so it is the array index of
/// this atlas everywhere in the render path.
pub index: usize,
/// Square layer size in pixels. Source images scale to it.
pub resolution: u32,
/// Groups, or a placeholder.
pub source: AtlasSource,
/// Base name of this atlas in the shipped asset tree, which is not
/// always `id`. The browser build fetches `packed/{pak_name}.pak` and
/// stores the bytes under `id`, because the deployed web tree holds
/// `structural.pak` and `assets.pak` for the atlases the engine calls
/// `structures` and `environment`. Native builds and `pack_assets` use
/// `id` on both sides, so they never need the field.
pub pak_name: String,
/// Texture directory for an atlas that declares no groups.
pub dir: Option<String>,
/// Side-car index this atlas needs, relative to the asset root.
pub manifest: Option<String>,
/// Texture families, in `type_id` order.
pub groups: Vec<AtlasGroupDesc>,
/// Every layer of this atlas is a TILE, not a sprite.
/// 
/// It decides two things at once, and neither is enough alone: the
/// sampler repeats instead of clamping, and the mip chain is reduced
/// over the torus instead of holding the edge texel. Repeat addressing
/// over a clamped mip chain tiles at mip zero and seams at every level
/// below it.
/// 
/// Per ATLAS and not per layer, because a sampler belongs to a set. An
/// application with both kinds declares two atlases, which the slot
/// scheme already supports.
pub tiling: bool,

/// Diagnostic label. Names the family in load logs and in validation
/// errors, and is the key the application reads `base_id` back with.
pub name: String,
/// The `type_id` of the first path.
pub base_id: u32,
/// Texture directories, relative to the asset root, in `type_id` order.
pub paths: Vec<String>,
/// Variant cap per directory. It differs between families, which is why
/// a family with two caps splits into two groups.
pub max_variants: u32,
/// Load normal maps for every path in this group.
pub normals: bool,
/// Load mask maps for every path in this group.
pub masks: bool,

/// Load the groups through `MegaAtlas::load_multi`.
Groups,
/// Build a 1×1 placeholder and ignore the groups. The slot exists so the
/// render path can bind something, and the real textures arrive by
/// another route.
Placeholder,

/// Lookup key, and the `PAK_STORE` key on the browser path.
pub id: String,
/// Directory under the asset root.
pub dir: String,
/// Base name in the shipped asset tree. See `AtlasDesc::pak_name`.
pub pak_name: String,
/// Load normal maps.
pub normals: bool,
/// Load mask maps.
pub masks: bool,
/// Square layer size the offline packer writes. It is not the runtime
/// resolution: `ImageAtlas::load` keeps the source size.
pub pack_resolution: u32,
/// Layer cap the offline packer writes.
pub pack_max_textures: u32,

/// Path to texture directory (contains albedo/, normal/, mask/ subdirs)
pub path: std::path::PathBuf,
/// Unique type identifier within this mega-atlas
pub type_id: u32,
/// Maximum variants to load from this directory
pub max_variants: u32,
/// Whether to load normal maps
pub load_normals: bool,
/// Whether to load mask maps
pub load_masks: bool,

/// Type identifier within the parent mega-atlas (e.g. raw ore base = 0)
pub type_id: u32,
/// Layer index of first variant in this group
pub base_offset: u32,
/// Number of texture variants
pub variant_count: u32,
/// Whether normal maps are included
pub has_normals: bool,
/// Whether mask maps are included
pub has_masks: bool,
/// Human-readable stem name (used for boot terminal display)
pub stem_name: String,

/// Reconstructed type_id → layer mapping
pub mapping: TextureMapping,
/// Pre-processed pixel data per layer (same layout as CachedLayer)
pub layers: Vec<CachedLayer>,
/// Stem names from manifest (for ship atlas boot display)
pub stem_names: Vec<String>,
/// Square texture resolution (e.g. 256)
pub resolution: u32,
/// True if any entry declared normals
pub has_normals: bool,
/// True if any entry declared masks
pub has_masks: bool,

/// The file could not be read.
Read { path: PathBuf, message: String },
/// The text is not valid TOML, or a field has the wrong type.
Syntax(String),
/// manifest_version names a format this loader does not implement.
Version { found: u32 },
/// The text parsed, and then broke one or more rules.
Invalid(Vec<String>),

pub manifest_version: u32,
pub app: App,
pub window: Window,
pub web: Option<Web>,
pub assets: Assets,
pub atlas: Vec<Atlas>,
pub image_atlas: Vec<ImageAtlas>,
pub runtime_env: Vec<RuntimeEnv>,
pub build: Build,
/// `[settings.*]` — what the project remembers about how it LOOKS.
/// 
/// A free table and not a typed struct, and that is the whole design. This
/// struct is `deny_unknown_fields`, so a section that is not declared here
/// does not fail to load, it fails to PARSE and takes the project with it.
/// One declared field means a subsystem adds a settings block by
/// registering it and never touches this file.
/// 
/// NOTHING IN THIS MODULE READS IT. `project_settings` owns every name
/// inside, the diff against the defaults, and the write back through
/// `toml_edit`. What is here is the declaration that keeps serde quiet.
pub settings: toml::Table,

pub package: String,
pub bin: String,
pub feature: Vec<Feature>,
pub target: Vec<Target>,
/// What each platform needs beyond a cargo argv.
/// 
/// A `[[build.target]]` says how to COMPILE. These say how to SHIP: which
/// slices are fused, what gets signed, what the archive is called, which
/// identifiers a store demands. The ultralite scripts held all of it as
/// shell constants, so no reader could see it and no panel could edit it.
pub macos: MacOs,
pub windows: Windows,
pub linux: Linux,
pub android: Android,

/// The `[[build.target]]` id that compiles this platform. Empty means the
/// panel guesses, the way `deploy::guess_source` already does.
pub target: String,
/// Name of the binary written into `out_dir`.
pub artifact: String,
/// Directory the artifact is written to, relative to the project root.
pub out_dir: String,
/// Build both slices and join them with lipo.
/// 
/// A single-slice binary runs on the machine that built it and on nothing
/// else, which is the failure that is invisible until somebody else opens
/// it.
pub universal: bool,
/// `MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET` for the Intel slice. The only statement of
/// the OS floor anywhere in the build.
pub deployment_target: String,
/// Sign the artifact. lipo invalidates the per-slice signatures, so an
/// unsigned fused binary is killed on launch with no message.
pub sign: bool,
/// Signing identity. `-` is ad-hoc, which is what the script uses.
pub identity: String,

pub target: String,
pub artifact: String,
pub out_dir: String,
/// Link the C runtime statically.
/// 
/// Without it the executable imports VCRUNTIME140.dll and will not start
/// on a machine that has no redistributable. The cost is 174 kB and about
/// 72 seconds of link time, which the script records.
pub static_crt: bool,
/// Which toolchain links it: `xwin` cross-compiles with clang-cl, `msvc`
/// is a native Windows host with the real linker.
pub toolchain: String,

pub target: String,
pub artifact: String,
pub out_dir: String,
/// How the build reaches a Linux toolchain: `zigbuild` links with zig on
/// this host, `container` runs it in docker through `cross`, `native` is
/// a real Linux machine.
/// 
/// `zigbuild` is the default because the graph no longer asks for a
/// distribution's development packages. wry was the eleven GTK and WebKit
/// ones and it is macOS-only now; rodio was libasound and it is behind the
/// `audio` feature. What is left compiles its own C or dlopens at run
/// time, and zig supplies the libc and the glibc floor.
pub route: String,
/// Oldest glibc the artifact may demand. Symbol versioning is one way:
/// a binary built against a newer glibc will not start on an older one.
pub glibc: String,
/// Archive format. tar keeps the executable bit under every extractor;
/// a zip keeps it too, but only until something on the other end ignores
/// the unix attributes.
pub format: String,

pub target: String,
/// Directory the APK is copied to, relative to the project root.
/// 
/// One folder per platform. Android had none and used the project root,
/// so the one artifact that is a FILE rather than a tree landed beside
/// `warp_app.toml`.
pub out_dir: String,
/// Application id, the name the device and the store key on. Empty means
/// the driver derives `com.warp.<app.id>.android`.
pub package: String,
/// The name under the icon. Empty means `app.name`.
pub label: String,
/// The project's renderer type, which the generated entry point calls
/// through `warp_core::run_android`.
pub renderer: String,
pub min_sdk: u32,
pub target_sdk: u32,
/// ABIs to build. The driver builds arm64 only today; every other entry
/// is declared and unbuilt until it grows the targets.
pub abis: Vec<String>,
/// `landscape`, `portrait` or `sensor`.
pub orientation: String,
/// Path to the signing keystore, relative to the project root.
/// 
/// THE PATH ONLY. The store password, the key password and everything
/// else that unlocks it are resolved at build time from the environment,
/// the OS keychain, or a prompt held for the session. None of them is a
/// field of this struct and none is ever written to this file.
pub keystore: String,
/// Which key inside the keystore signs. A name, not a secret.
pub key_alias: String,

pub id: String,
/// None means the host triple.
pub triple: Option<String>,
pub profile: String,
pub default_features: bool,
pub features: Vec<String>,
pub capabilities: Vec<String>,
/// Program that builds this target in place of cargo. None makes the
/// target a cargo target, which is what every target was before.
/// 
/// A script composes what a cargo argv cannot: ./run.sh builds, then
/// launches the binary with the developer argv.
pub command: Option<String>,
/// Arguments for `command`. It has no meaning without one, and rule 12
/// reports the pair the other way round.
pub args: Vec<String>,
/// Label for a launch button on the editor's transport strip, or None to
/// keep the target off it.
/// 
/// One field carries both the membership and the words, because the two
/// cannot be allowed to disagree: a boolean would need a second field for
/// the label, and a bare label list somewhere else would name targets by
/// id from outside the table that declares them.
/// 
/// The engine holds no list of which ids are worth a button. `run` and
/// `run-debug` mean nothing to warp_core, and a toolbar that knew those
/// names would be an editor with one project's vocabulary compiled into
/// it. The project says which of its entry points are worth one click.
pub toolbar: Option<String>,
/// Which drawn glyph the button carries: `launch` or `launch-debug`.
/// 
/// A name from a closed set and not a path to an image, because these are
/// shapes the engine draws — there is no font and no atlas behind them,
/// which is exactly why they stay crisp at every UI scale. A project
/// picks from the set; it cannot add to it.
/// 
/// Defaults to `launch`. Unread without `toolbar`, and rule 13 says so.
pub toolbar_icon: Option<String>,
pub entry: Option<String>,
pub out_dir: Option<String>,
pub bindgen_target: Option<String>,

/// Behind everything. The void a scene sits in.
Background,
/// A panel, a card, a dialog — the body of a thing laid over the scene.
Surface,
/// A surface lifted off the one under it: a button, a header, a tab.
SurfaceRaised,
/// A surface pressed into the one under it: a field, a well, a track.
SurfaceSunken,
/// Body text and iconography on a surface.
Ink,
/// Secondary text: a caption, a hint, a disabled label.
InkDim,
/// A heading, or the one line that must be read first.
InkStrong,
/// Ink for a surface drawn in the accent colour, where normal ink fails.
InkInverted,
/// This one. Selection, focus, the hovered row, the live note.
Highlight,
/// The game's own colour. Used sparingly, or it stops meaning anything.
Accent,
/// It worked.
Ok,
/// It worked, with something to say about it.
Warn,
/// It did not work.
Bad,
/// A rule between two areas, and the border of a box.
Line,
/// A rule that has to be seen: a focused border, an axis, a baseline.
LineStrong,

/// Corner radius. Zero is a square box, and squares are a look.
Rounding,
/// Border width. Zero draws no border at all.
Border,
/// Space between a box's border and its content, horizontally.
PadX,
/// The same, vertically.
PadY,
/// Space between one box and the next.
Gap,

items.doc:
The colour a role carries right now.

This is the call a game makes. One relaxed load, no branch, no allocation.

ctx.draw_rect(min, max, palette::color(Role::Surface).bytes());
ui.label(RichText::new("score").color(palette::color(Role::Ink).egui()));

A themed box for a HUD, on the `Surface` role.

palette::frame().show(ui, |ui| {
ui.label("score");
});

