A shot to `point` with the default timing.

```
use warp_sdk::camera::Shot;
use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let shot = Shot::to(Vec2::new(5.0, 0.0));
assert!(shot.scale.is_none());
```

Set the zoom scale at the point.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Shot; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let shot = Shot::to(Vec2::zero()).zoom(2.0);
```

Set the seconds of the pan to the point and of the pan back.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Shot; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let shot = Shot::to(Vec2::zero()).travel(1.5, 1.0);
```

Set the seconds at the point.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Shot; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let shot = Shot::to(Vec2::zero()).hold(3.0);
```

Show letterbox bars of this fraction of the screen height. A value
of 0.1 is a good cinematic look.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Shot; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let shot = Shot::to(Vec2::zero()).letterbox(0.1);
```

The full length of the shot in seconds.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Shot; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
assert!(Shot::to(Vec2::zero()).duration() > 0.0);
```

The final view of one frame: what the renderer gets. Get it from
[`Camera2d::view`].

```
use warp_sdk::camera::View2d;
use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let v = View2d { pos: Vec2::zero(), scale: 1.0, rotation: 0.0, viewport: [200, 100] };
assert_eq!(v.world_to_screen(Vec2::zero()), Vec2::new(100.0, 50.0));
```

