The half extents of the visible world, before rotation, world units.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::View2d; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let v = View2d { pos: Vec2::zero(), scale: 2.0, rotation: 0.0, viewport: [200, 100] };
assert_eq!(v.half_extents(), Vec2::new(4.0, 2.0));
```

Pixels per world unit.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::View2d; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let v = View2d { pos: Vec2::zero(), scale: 2.0, rotation: 0.0, viewport: [200, 100] };
assert_eq!(v.pixels_per_unit(), 25.0);
```

A world position to a screen pixel, origin top left, y down. This is
the transform of the engine scene shaders and `lit2d`.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::View2d; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let v = View2d { pos: Vec2::zero(), scale: 1.0, rotation: 0.0, viewport: [200, 100] };
assert_eq!(v.world_to_screen(Vec2::new(0.0, 1.0)), Vec2::new(100.0, 0.0));
```

A screen pixel to a world position. The inverse of
[`View2d::world_to_screen`]. Use it for the mouse.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::View2d; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let v = View2d { pos: Vec2::zero(), scale: 1.0, rotation: 0.0, viewport: [200, 100] };
assert_eq!(v.screen_to_world(Vec2::new(100.0, 50.0)), Vec2::zero());
```

True if a world position shows on the screen.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::View2d; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let v = View2d { pos: Vec2::zero(), scale: 1.0, rotation: 0.0, viewport: [200, 100] };
assert!(v.contains(Vec2::new(1.5, 0.5)));
```

The 2D game camera. See the module header for the frame order.

```
use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d;
use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let mut cam = Camera2d::new([1280, 720]);
cam.snap_to(Vec2::new(3.0, 1.0));
cam.update(1.0 / 60.0, Vec2::new(3.1, 1.0));
let view = cam.view();
```

