items.doc:
A camera at the world origin, scale 1, for a viewport in pixels.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d;
let cam = Camera2d::new([1280, 720]);
assert_eq!(cam.scale(), 1.0);
```

Set the viewport size in pixels. Call it when the window changes
size, for example from `Renderer::input`.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d;
let mut cam = Camera2d::new([1280, 720]);
cam.set_viewport([1920, 1080]);
assert_eq!(cam.viewport(), [1920, 1080]);
```

The viewport size in pixels.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d;
assert_eq!(Camera2d::new([640, 360]).viewport(), [640, 360]);
```

Move the camera to `pos` with no glide and stop all motion. Use it on
the first frame and after a teleport or a level change.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let mut cam = Camera2d::new([1280, 720]);
cam.snap_to(Vec2::new(5.0, 2.0));
assert_eq!(cam.view().pos, Vec2::new(5.0, 2.0));
```

Set the zoom target, the half view height in world units. The zoom
eases to it over `zoom_smooth_time`.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d;
let mut cam = Camera2d::new([1280, 720]);
cam.set_zoom(4.0);
assert_eq!(cam.zoom_target(), 4.0);
```

The zoom target.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d;
assert_eq!(Camera2d::new([1280, 720]).zoom_target(), 1.0);
```

The zoom now, before a shot changes it.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d;
assert_eq!(Camera2d::new([1280, 720]).scale(), 1.0);
```

The scale that shows every point with a `margin` fraction of space
around them, at the base rotation of this camera. The result does
not obey `min_scale` and `max_scale`.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let cam = Camera2d::new([200, 100]);
let s = cam.fit_scale(&[Vec2::new(-4.0, 0.0), Vec2::new(4.0, 0.0)], 0.0);
assert_eq!(s, 2.0);
```

One frame of follow. `target` is the world position to follow.
Call it once per frame, before you draw.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let mut cam = Camera2d::new([1280, 720]);
cam.update(1.0 / 60.0, Vec2::new(1.0, 0.0));
```

One frame that frames a group: the target is the centre of the
points, and the zoom eases to fit them all with a `margin` fraction
of space. Use it for co-op, a boss fight or a battle overview.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let mut cam = Camera2d::new([1280, 720]);
let players = [Vec2::new(-3.0, 0.0), Vec2::new(6.0, 2.0)];
cam.update_group(1.0 / 60.0, &players, 0.2);
```

Add screen shake. `amount` adds to the trauma, which stays in 0 to 1.
About 0.2 is a small hit, 0.5 a large hit, 1.0 an explosion.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d;
let mut cam = Camera2d::new([1280, 720]);
cam.add_trauma(0.5);
assert_eq!(cam.trauma(), 0.5);
```

The trauma now, 0 to 1.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d;
assert_eq!(Camera2d::new([1280, 720]).trauma(), 0.0);
```

The shake of the last frame: translation in world units and rotation
in radians.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d;
let (offset, angle) = Camera2d::new([1280, 720]).shake_now();
assert_eq!(angle, 0.0);
```

Start a cinematic shot. A new shot replaces the shot that plays.
Follow continues below the shot, so the return goes to where the
target is now.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::{Camera2d, Shot}; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let mut cam = Camera2d::new([1280, 720]);
cam.play(Shot::to(Vec2::new(20.0, 3.0)).hold(2.0).letterbox(0.1));
assert!(cam.shot_active());
```

Stop the shot at once. For a smooth exit, let the shot finish.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::{Camera2d, Shot}; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let mut cam = Camera2d::new([1280, 720]);
cam.play(Shot::to(Vec2::zero()));
cam.cut_shot();
assert!(!cam.shot_active());
```

True while a shot plays. Use it to lock the player input.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d;
assert!(!Camera2d::new([1280, 720]).shot_active());
```

The height of each letterbox bar now, as a fraction of the screen
height. It eases in and out with the shot.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d;
assert_eq!(Camera2d::new([1280, 720]).letterbox(), 0.0);
```

The final view of the last `update`.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d;
let v = Camera2d::new([1280, 720]).view();
assert_eq!(v.scale, 1.0);
```

A world position to a screen pixel, with the view of the last update.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let cam = Camera2d::new([200, 100]);
assert_eq!(cam.world_to_screen(Vec2::zero()), Vec2::new(100.0, 50.0));
```

A screen pixel to a world position, with the view of the last update.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d; use warp_sdk::ultraviolet::Vec2;
let cam = Camera2d::new([200, 100]);
assert_eq!(cam.screen_to_world(Vec2::new(100.0, 50.0)), Vec2::zero());
```

Send the view to the engine renderer: position, scale and rotation.

```ignore
fn render(&mut self, _v: ViewportId, ctx: &mut RenderContext) {
self.cam.update(dt, self.player);
self.cam.apply(ctx);
}
```

Write the view into an engine `View`. The engine stores the position
relative to the render origin, so this subtracts the origin.

```
# use warp_sdk::camera::Camera2d;
let mut v = warp_sdk::types::View::default();
Camera2d::new([1280, 720]).apply_to_view(&mut v);
assert_eq!(v.scale, 1.0);
