The link to a real gamepad library. Implement it to add a backend.

```ignore
struct MyPad { /* a device handle */ }
impl PadBackend for MyPad {
fn poll(&mut self) -> PadState { /* read events, fill PadState */ PadState::default() }
fn rumble(&mut self, r: Rumble) { /* force feedback */ }
}
```

The real gamepad backend, on the `gilrs` crate. Desktop only.

It follows every connected pad. [`PadBackend::poll`] gives the state of
the pad that sent the last input, so a second player who picks up a pad
takes over. The backend sees a pad that is plugged in or pulled out
while the game runs. Rumble uses gilrs force feedback on the active pad,
where the pad and the platform support it.

Most games call [`default_backend`] and do not name this type.

```no_run
use warp_sdk::gamepad::{GilrsPad, PadBackend};
if let Some(mut pad) = GilrsPad::new() {
let state = pad.poll();
}
```

The best backend for this platform, boxed: [`GilrsPad`] on the desktop
when gilrs starts, else [`NoPad`]. Call it once, in `Game::new`, and keep
the box.

```ignore
use warp_sdk::gamepad::{self, PadBackend, Verbs};
struct Game { verbs: Verbs, pad: Box<dyn PadBackend> }
// In Game::new:
let pad = gamepad::default_backend();
// In Game::input:
self.verbs.update(input, self.pad.as_mut());
```

Keeps a press for a short window, so a press made a little early still
